WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.380
This is a sixteen-line LiDAR on a motor, its sweeps stacked as they arrive.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:05.502
A ball of streaks.

00:00:05.882 --> 00:00:08.962
Turn the sensor while it spins and every return is measured

00:00:08.962 --> 00:00:10.082
from a different angle.

00:00:13.002 --> 00:00:13.604
I'm Swivel.

00:00:13.604 --> 00:00:17.215
Here is why alpha-LiDAR turns an ordinary LiDAR, and what its code

00:00:17.215 --> 00:00:18.419
does to keep up.

00:00:21.075 --> 00:00:23.220
Unroll the sphere around the sensor.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:26.795
Upright, sixteen lines cover a band about thirty degrees tall.

00:00:27.215 --> 00:00:30.749
Lay it on its side and each revolution becomes a vertical fan.

00:00:31.169 --> 00:00:32.833
Now let the motor turn it.

00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:35.606
Within a second the fan has passed through every direction.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:40.251
Swing it back and forth instead, and the returns concentrate where you point it.

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:44.584
The head takes several LiDARs.

00:00:44.584 --> 00:00:47.524
A gimbal motor with a built-in encoder turns it.

00:00:47.904 --> 00:00:51.026
The IMU rides on the head with the LiDAR, so it feels

00:00:51.026 --> 00:00:52.066
the motor's turn directly.

00:00:53.433 --> 00:00:57.401
Integrate the IMU through the sweep and move every return to the

00:00:57.401 --> 00:00:59.055
pose at the sweep's end.

00:00:59.055 --> 00:01:00.377
That is FAST-LIO2's deskew.

00:01:00.777 --> 00:01:04.720
Each return carries a covariance, and an angle error grows with range.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:08.787
Far, uncertain returns count less when they are matched to the plane map.

00:01:09.187 --> 00:01:11.834
The motor encoder never enters the filter.

00:01:11.834 --> 00:01:15.615
It only re-expresses the pose for whatever carries the head.

00:01:16.817 --> 00:01:20.912
Its first change: start while the head is already spinning, using the

00:01:20.912 --> 00:01:22.277
orientation the IMU reports.

00:01:22.697 --> 00:01:26.467
Its second: the motor angle, used only to give the pose of the base.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:31.454
The repo's own run: about seventeen milliseconds a scan.

00:01:31.834 --> 00:01:35.851
And six centimetres of error, averaged over windows of two hundred poses,

00:01:35.851 --> 00:01:37.525
each aligned on its own.

00:01:37.525 --> 00:01:38.529
A kind metric.

00:01:39.842 --> 00:01:44.524
On a synthetic head already spinning when the recording starts, alpha-LiDAR holds

00:01:44.524 --> 00:01:48.035
a few centimetres up to six radians a second.

00:01:48.035 --> 00:01:51.936
FAST-LIO2, starting the usual static way, drifts off by kilometres.

00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:57.204
Take the motor away and it is still a good odometry: four

00:01:57.204 --> 00:02:01.275
point four six centimetres on an Oxford walk, ahead of FAST-LIO2 and

00:02:01.275 --> 00:02:02.971
PV-LIO on the same machine.

00:02:04.475 --> 00:02:07.275
Turn the sensor, keep the IMU on the head, and the

00:02:07.275 --> 00:02:08.293
same filter keeps up.

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.109
A LiDAR on a motor, and the filter that keeps up with it.

00:02:15.109 --> 00:02:15.849
Thanks for watching!

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.602
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:18.602 --> 00:02:19.202
I'm Swivel.

00:02:19.202 --> 00:02:19.503
Bye!
